= Computer security — Technical =

== Security model ==
A security analysis can be organized as:

<pre>
assets
+ adversary capabilities
+ trust assumptions
+ attack surface
→ required security properties
</pre>

Without a threat model, claims such as “secure” are underspecified.

== Reference objectives ==
Confidentiality, integrity and availability form the classic CIA triad, but modern systems also require authentication, authorization, accountability, provenance, isolation and recoverability.

== Trusted computing base ==
The trusted computing base is the set of components whose correct behavior is necessary for enforcement of a security policy.

Reducing its size can simplify assurance but does not remove external dependencies.

== Vulnerability and exploit ==
A vulnerability is a weakness that can violate a security policy under relevant conditions.

An exploit is a technique or artifact that uses a vulnerability.

Risk additionally depends on likelihood, exposure, impact and compensating controls.

== Compositional limits ==
Individually secure primitives do not automatically compose into a secure protocol/system.

Interfaces, side channels, key lifecycle, randomness, state, concurrency and human behavior can introduce failures.

